window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][19] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What decision rule would you use between the custom and standard date picker? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Whether the control is the core of the experience. If dates are the central action, as in a travel search, a custom picker may justify its cost, if it passes a test: task time no worse than the standard, error rate no higher, and no accessibility regressions on screen reader and keyboard use. If dates are a supporting control, use the standard one. I would run a quick prototype test with eight users on both, plus an accessibility check, and decide on the numbers. The rule is written before the test so the loudest opinion does not win.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you keep the relationship with the designer and engineer intact? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By treating it as a shared problem and not a contest. I would thank the designer for the aim (a distinctive feel), ask the engineer for the cost of each option and the maintenance burden, and propose the compromise: standard component by default, custom styling where the standard allows, custom behavior only where tested. I would make sure the decision and its reason are written down and that the designer sees what would change it. If the standard is chosen, the designer gets another place to express the feel, so that the outcome does not read as a loss.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c10-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C10-05 · Hard · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> The growth team wants a pre-checked box that opts users into marketing emails. They expect opt-in to more than double. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do it, since it doubles opt-in,&quot; or &quot;refuse&quot; with no reasoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Applies an ethical test, mentions the legal and trust risk, and proposes an alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies net outcomes (engaged recipients, complaints), proposes a better-timed ask, sets guardrails and says how to take the conversation to the growth team.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd start by taking the growth team's goal seriously: they want more people who receive and value marketing messages. Then I'd run the ethical nudge test (FW-C10-06). A pre-checked box is not transparent (many people do not notice it), is easy to leave only if you spot it, is not something users would endorse if they understood, and is not symmetric. It is a dark pattern. In several jurisdictions, consent rules require an affirmative action to opt in, so it may also be unlawful <code>[V: GDPR Article 4(11) (clear affirmative action) and the ePrivacy Directive (opt-in for marketing email), as summarized in guidance]</code>, and I'd ask legal and privacy teams immediately.</p>\n<p><em>Count net outcomes.</em> Suppose an unchecked box gets 18% opt-in and a pre-checked box 41% (invented). The pre-checked list is bigger, but its members did not choose it: say 9% of them open the emails, against 31% of the people who chose. Engaged recipients per 100 sign-ups: 18 × 0.31 = 5.6 versus 41 × 0.09 = 3.7. The honest approach produces more engaged recipients. The pre-checked list also generates more unsubscribes and spam complaints, which damage the reputation of our sending domain and lower delivery to everyone.</p>\n<p><em>An alternative that serves the goal.</em> Ask at a better moment, with a clear value: after the first order, &quot;Want order updates and occasional offers? Yes / No thanks,&quot; unchecked, with an easy way to change it later. Test the wording and timing. Measure engaged recipients per 100 sign-ups, complaints and unsubscribes, with opt-in rate as a diagnostic and not the target (<a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, C05).</p>\n<p><em>How I'd say it.</em> &quot;Opt-in is the wrong number. The right number is engaged subscribers, and the checkbox lowers it while raising legal risk. Let's test a better ask.&quot; What would change my mind: if the test showed that the honest ask gets too few opt-ins to matter, I'd look at the value we are offering, not at hiding the choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would you propose instead of the pre-checked box? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;An unchecked box with a clear value statement, shown at a moment when people understand why they might want it, such as after the first successful purchase: Get order updates and offers? with one tap to say yes and one to decline. I would test it against the pre-checked version and compare engaged recipients (opens and clicks in 60 days), unsubscribes and spam complaints, not just opt-in rate. A pre-checked box may double opt-ins, but if half of them never open and 3% mark messages as spam, deliverability falls and the list is worth less.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you take this conversation to the growth team? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;As a shared goal. I would agree that we want more recipients who value the messages, show the numbers from a test (opt-ins, engaged recipients, complaints, deliverability), and explain the legal and trust risk in plain terms: consent rules in several regions require an affirmative action, and complaints can reduce the reach of all our emails. I would offer an alternative that raises opt-in through better timing and value, and keep guardrails on complaints. If leadership still wants the pre-checked box, I would escalate the legal question to counsel, since it is a risk beyond my authority to accept.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c10-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C10-06 · Hard · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Leadership wants to skip accessibility work to make the launch date. What do you say?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Accessibility is the law&quot; or &quot;okay, we'll do it later.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains the users affected, the cost of retrofitting, and a minimum bar.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the audience, separates launch-blocking items from later improvements, notes legal exposure and brand risk, offers a dated plan and a scope trade.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd treat it as a scoping conversation with data. First, who is affected: people who use screen readers, voice control, larger text, captions or switch devices, and, by the inclusive-design framing, people with temporary and situational limits (a broken arm, bright sun, a noisy room). Suppose we estimate that 8% of our users use assistive features or larger text (invented), which is 80,000 of a million. That is not an edge case.</p>\n<p><em>Cost.</em> Retrofitting accessibility after launch usually costs far more than building it in, because it touches structure (labels, focus order, contrast, targets) that is expensive to change once shipped. Skipping it also creates risk: legal exposure in some markets <code>[V: consent and children's data rules differ by country (GDPR, COPPA, India's DPDP Act)]</code>, app-store and partner requirements, and brand damage when people who cannot use the product say so.</p>\n<p><em>A minimum bar that does not threaten the date.</em> Launch-blocking items: labels for every control and image for screen readers, contrast of 4.5:1 for normal text (a designer can check in minutes), touch targets of adequate size, keyboard and voice operation of the core path, and no information carried by color alone. These are small if built in from the start. Defer to a dated plan: enhancements to advanced features, deeper testing with assistive-technology users, and nonessential motion refinements.</p>\n<p><em>What I'd offer.</em> &quot;We can hold the date if we protect the core-path basics, which add about three days, and defer the advanced items to a dated plan with an owner. If we skip the basics we will spend more later and risk exclusion on day one.&quot; I'd bring a trade: cut a non-core feature before cutting accessibility. What would change my mind: if the launch is a small, internal pilot with no public users, I'd allow a later bar for the pilot, and I'd write down the date by which it must meet the standard.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you separate launch-blocking accessibility items from later improvements? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By impact on task completion. Blockers are things that prevent a user with assistive technology from completing a core task: missing labels on controls, keyboard traps, insufficient contrast on key text, targets too small to hit, and content that cannot be read by a screen reader. Later items are enhancements: refined focus order on rarely used screens, improved captions styling, extra shortcuts. I would run a quick audit with automated tools plus a manual pass by someone who uses a screen reader, list defects by severity, and agree with engineering which are blockers. The list and its dates go to leadership.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What do you offer leadership instead of skipping the work? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A dated plan with a scope trade. Ship on time with the blockers fixed and a smaller feature set, or keep the full scope and move the date by one sprint. I would show the numbers: if 8% of users use assistive features or larger text, that is 80,000 of a million, retrofitting after launch costs more than building in, and legal and brand exposure are real. Then ask for the decision. A choice presented as two options with costs is easier to accept than a refusal, and I would put the accepted risks in writing.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c10-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C10-07 · Hard · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> In a usability test, two of five people failed a critical task. The team says five is too small a sample to act on. How do you respond?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Five is enough,&quot; or &quot;we need more users.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains what the test is for (finding problems) and what it cannot do (sizing them).</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives the discovery argument and the interval, separates the existence of a problem from its rate, proposes analytics and a cheap retest, and decides on cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The team is right about one thing and wrong about another. A five-person test cannot tell us how common the failure is: two of five is 40%, but the 95% interval for that is roughly 12% to 77%, so the true rate could be small or large. We should not report &quot;40% of users fail.&quot;</p>\n<p>But the purpose of the test is to find problems, not to count them. If each participant finds about 31% of the problems on average (the commonly reported figure <code>[V: Nielsen and Landauer (1993): one user finds about 31 percent of problems]</code>), five participants find about 84% of them (1 − 0.69⁵). Seeing two people fail a critical task, and seeing why, is strong evidence that a problem exists and what causes it. The question is not whether it exists; it does. The question is how much it matters and how cheaply we can fix it.</p>\n<p><em>What I'd do.</em> First, look at the cause: if both failures came from the same visible issue (a hidden control, an unclear label), the fix is probably cheap, and I'd fix it without waiting. Second, size it with analytics: what share of live users reach that step and abandon, and how does it compare with other steps? Third, run another round with five new participants after the fix, since people who failed now know the answer.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> If the fix costs a day, make it now. If it costs a month, size the problem first with analytics and weigh it against other work. I'd tell the team: &quot;Five people cannot give us a rate, and they have shown us a real problem and its cause. Let's fix the cheap part and measure the rest.&quot; What would change my mind: if the two failures had different causes, or if analytics showed almost no one reaches that step.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you separate whether a problem exists from how common it is? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Two failures out of five tell me with high confidence that the problem exists and tell me little about its rate. The interval for 2 of 5 is about 12% to 77%. For the rate, I would use analytics: the funnel for that task across all users, which shows the share who drop at that step, and a larger unmoderated test. For the existence question, a reproducible failure with a clear cause (for example, a button that looks disabled) is evidence enough to fix, and I would say so in the report while stating the rate as unknown.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you decide whether to fix it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;On cost and impact. Estimate the share of users on the critical task (from analytics), the failure rate range, and the cost of the fix. If the task is critical, the plausible failure rate is meaningful, and the fix takes two days, fix it and retest with five more users: a fix that costs less than the uncertainty is not worth debating. If the fix is large, run the analytics check first to see the rate. The decision rule: fix now if the fix is under a week and the task is critical; otherwise measure first, with a date.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c10-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Six exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked, and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c10-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C10-01 · Review a device-settings screen</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A device's settings screen (described, invented) has 40 switches in one scrolling list, a switch labeled &quot;Allow&quot; with no object, a &quot;Restore defaults&quot; button next to &quot;Save&quot; that acts without confirmation, gray-on-white labels at a contrast of 3.0 to 1, and a custom control for brightness that works differently from the device's other sliders. Review it against the ten heuristics and the platform's published guidelines as a reference, rank by severity and propose a fix order.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The setting is a device-maker's settings screen; no claim is made about any real product. Platform guidelines are a public reference <code>[V: Apple Human Interface Guidelines and Material Design]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The heuristic review (FW-C10-01) and the accessibility pass (FW-C10-03).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (1) &quot;Restore defaults&quot; next to &quot;Save&quot; with no confirmation or undo: user control and error prevention; blocks (data loss); fix first: separate it, confirm, or offer undo. (2) 40 switches in one list: recognition over recall, minimal design, cognitive load; slows; group by purpose and add search. (3) &quot;Allow&quot; with no object: match with the real world, visibility; slows; label &quot;Allow notifications from this app.&quot; (4) Labels at 3.0:1: accessibility (contrast needs 4.5:1 for normal text); blocks for some; darken to at least #767676 on white (4.54:1). (5) A custom brightness control that differs from the other sliders: consistency and standards, and the platform's guidelines suggest using the standard slider; annoys; use the standard control unless the custom one tests better. Order: 1, 4, 2, 3, 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Rank the contrast issue first if the audience includes many older users, since it blocks reading.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Fixing everything takes time; the order by severity puts irreversible harm and exclusion first.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c10-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C10-02 · A state map for photo upload</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A photo-upload feature lets a user pick a photo, upload it to a profile and see it appear. List all eight states (FW-C10-02) and what the user should see in each.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Mobile app, variable connection.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The state map.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Ideal: the photo appears with a confirmation. Empty: no photo yet; show a placeholder and &quot;Add a photo.&quot; Loading: a progress indicator with percentage and a way to cancel. Partial: if the thumbnail fails, show the text and a placeholder; upload continues. Error: &quot;Upload failed. Check your connection. Your photo is saved; try again&quot; with a retry button. Offline or slow: queue the upload and say &quot;Will upload when you're back online.&quot; Permission denied: if photo access was denied, explain why it is needed and link to settings. Success: &quot;Photo updated&quot; with Undo.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a state for &quot;too large a file&quot; with a message and an automatic resize.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Every state costs design and build time; the error, offline and permission states are the ones that most often cause abandonment.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c10-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C10-03 · Contrast ratios</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Using the usual minimums (4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text), classify these text colors on a white background, given measured ratios: (a) #767676 at 4.54; (b) #777777 at 4.48; (c) #0066CC at 5.57; (d) #808080 at 3.95; (e) #959595 at 3.0. For each, say whether it passes for normal text and for large text.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A designer asks for quick feedback.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Three minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The accessibility pass (FW-C10-03).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Passes both (4.54 ≥ 4.5). (b) Fails normal text by a hair (4.48 &lt; 4.5); passes large text. (c) Passes both. (d) Fails normal text (3.95 &lt; 4.5); passes large text (≥ 3). (e) Fails normal text; passes large text only at exactly 3.0, with no margin. The practical advice: choose colors with margin (for example, #6B6B6B at 5.33 rather than #767676), because rendering and screens vary.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Recommend that the design system define a small set of approved text colors that pass, so individual designers do not need to check.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Higher contrast is less subtle for the brand; accessible palettes are a design constraint, and the curb-cut effect means they also help in bright light.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c10-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C10-04 · Read a five-user test</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Five users try three tasks: (a) find a product, 5 of 5 succeed; (b) apply a coupon, 1 of 5 succeeds; (c) change delivery address, 3 of 5 succeed. Rank the problems, say what you can and cannot conclude about rates, and say what to do next.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A prototype checkout.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above. The 95% interval for 4 failures out of 5 is roughly 38% to 96%; for 2 failures out of 5, roughly 12% to 77%.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The five-user test (FW-C10-04).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Rank: (b) coupon, four of five failed, the biggest; (c) address, two of five failed; (a) fine. Conclusions: we can say the coupon task has a severe problem, and the observations should tell why (where people looked, what they tried). We cannot say that &quot;80% of customers fail&quot;: the interval is about 38% to 96%. Next: fix the cause, measure with analytics the share who reach and abandon at the coupon step on the live product, and retest with five new users.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If the coupon problem is cheap to fix and the address problem is expensive, fix the coupon at once and size the address issue first.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Waiting to size every problem delays obvious fixes; fixing without checking the cause risks missing it.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c10-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C10-05 · Spot the dark patterns</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Name the pattern and propose an honest alternative: (a) a free trial that converts to a paid plan with no reminder; (b) a &quot;No thanks, I prefer paying full price&quot; button; (c) a fee added on the last step of checkout; (d) a cancel link in tiny gray text on the fifth screen; (e) a pop-up that reappears every visit after being dismissed.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A design review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The ethical nudge test (FW-C10-06) and the dark-pattern table (C10.10).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Forced continuity: send a reminder before the trial ends and say the price at sign-up. (b) Confirmshaming: neutral wording (&quot;No thanks&quot;). (c) Hidden costs: show the total from the start. (d) Hard to cancel with misdirection: a clear cancel link in the account page and a short flow. (e) Nagging: respect the dismissal for a long period or ask a different question later.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> For (a), offer a &quot;remind me&quot; option at sign-up and a one-click cancel in the reminder.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Honest versions cost some short-term conversion and reduce complaints, refunds and risk; measure net outcomes.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c10-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C10-06 · Set the quality bar</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For each feature say the quality bar for works, usable and delightful, and what you accept as rough: (a) an internal admin report used by five staff; (b) the payment screen of a consumer app; (c) a first-time-user onboarding tour for a new product.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Limited time in a sprint.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The quality dial (FW-C10-05).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Works: high. Usable: adequate. Delightful: low; accept a plain layout, no animations. (b) Works: very high. Usable: high; clear errors, honest totals, accessibility. Delightful: moderate, with the confirmation moment (peak-end rule). Accept rough: nothing in the core path. (c) Works: high. Usable: high; short, skippable, with a clear next step. Delightful: moderate to high since first impressions shape retention; accept a less polished second language version if it follows a date.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> For (c), test a lighter onboarding (one tip) against the tour before investing in polish.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A lower bar is faster; record the accepted rough edges and a date to revisit them.</p>\n<span id=\"c10-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for two weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: see and name.</em> Take three screens from products you use. For each, spend ten minutes on the heuristic review (FW-C10-01) and the state map (FW-C10-02), naming each problem with its principle and cost. Use IQ-C10-02 and EX-C10-01.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: judge and decide.</em> Use IQ-C10-03, IQ-C10-05, IQ-C10-06, IQ-C10-07 and EX-C10-05. Practice the quality dial, the ethical nudge test and the accessibility minimum bar aloud, with a partner who pushes back with deadlines and metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;conversion would go up&quot; at some point, and answer with the ethical nudge test and a net outcome. Practice saying &quot;I'd test that with five people&quot; in a sentence.</p>\n<p><strong>Observe real users once.</strong> Run a five-person usability test of any product, with three tasks. Record where people hesitate. Nothing replaces watching.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I start from the user's task?</li>\n<li>Did I name each problem with a principle and its cost?</li>\n<li>Did I check the states beyond the ideal?</li>\n<li>Did I raise accessibility with specific barriers and fixes?</li>\n<li>Did I set a quality bar by risk and say what I accept?</li>\n<li>Did I run the ethical nudge test on any steering design?</li>\n<li>Did I propose a test and a metric with a guardrail?</li>\n<li>Did I finish with the two-sentence close from section 4?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the product, the top three problems with principles, the state you missed and your score out of ten.</p>\n<span id=\"c10-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next two weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Run the heuristic review and the state map on one screen of your product. Share the list with the designer, ranked by severity, and ask what they would add.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Run the accessibility pass (FW-C10-03) on one flow: check contrast, labels, target size and keyboard operation. Fix one cheap issue and log the rest.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Watch five users, or five recordings, attempt one task in your product. Write down each hesitation. Rank by severity and share with your team.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Look for a flow in your product that fails the ethical nudge test (a pre-checked box, a hard-to-find cancel, a nag). Write the net-outcome case for changing it and the honest alternative.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Write a quality bar (FW-C10-05) for the next feature: where you will spend and what you will accept as rough, with a date to revisit.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the ranked heuristic review (a time you found and prioritized usability problems), the five-user observation (a time you watched users and changed a decision), and the net-outcome case against a dark pattern (a time you protected users and the business). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c10-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c10-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The PM owns the problem and the outcome, the designer owns the craft; give criteria, not layouts, and settle disagreements with a quick test.</li>\n<li>Name usability problems with a principle and a cost, and review the states beyond the ideal: empty, loading, error, offline, permission denied.</li>\n<li>Treat accessibility as part of the main path: a short pass on contrast, labels, targets and non-color cues catches most barriers, and it helps everyone.</li>\n<li>Set the quality bar by risk: spend on the core task, trust moments and accessibility; accept recorded rough edges elsewhere.</li>\n<li>Apply the ethical nudge test to any design that steers behavior, and measure net outcomes, because a pattern that lifts one number can hurt the one that matters.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c10-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Don Norman, <em>The Design of Everyday Things</em> (revised edition, 2013). Affordances, signifiers, feedback and the conceptual model.</li>\n<li>Steve Krug, <em>Don't Make Me Think</em> (revised edition, 2014). Short and practical on usability and testing.</li>\n<li>Jakob Nielsen, &quot;10 Usability Heuristics for User Interface Design,&quot; Nielsen Norman Group <code>[V: Nielsen Norman Group article; heuristics developed with Rolf Molich (1990), refined by Nielsen in 1994 from 249 usability problems, article updated 2020]</code>. The source for FW-C10-01.</li>\n<li>World Wide Web Consortium, Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG), current version 2.2 <code>[V: W3C, WCAG 2.2 Recommendation (latest dated version 12 December 2024)]</code>. The public standard behind FW-C10-03.</li>\n<li>Apple, <em>Human Interface Guidelines</em>; Google, <em>Material Design</em>; Microsoft, Fluent design and the Inclusive Design toolkit. Public platform references <code>[V: Apple Human Interface Guidelines and Material Design; Fluent not rechecked]</code>.</li>\n<li>Kat Holmes, <em>Mismatch: How Inclusion Shapes Design</em> (2018). Inclusive design and the idea of mismatch.</li>\n<li>Richard H. Thaler and Cass R. Sunstein, <em>Nudge</em> (2008). Choice architecture and its ethics.</li>\n<li>Harry Brignull and others, deceptive.design (formerly darkpatterns.org). A catalog of dark patterns <code>[V: Brignull coined dark patterns in 2010; the site is now deceptive.design]</code>.</li>\n<li>US Federal Trade Commission, press release on the Amazon Prime settlement (25 September 2025) and the FTC's refunds page for the settlement (read); the complaint filed June 2023 and amended 20 September 2023 (known through the FTC's announcements and press summaries, not read directly). Sources for CS-C10-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c11\"></span>\n<h2>C11 — Product Critique and Teardown Method</h2>\n<span id=\"c11-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;What is your favorite product, and what would you change?&quot; and &quot;Critique this app&quot; are among the most open questions in a product loop, and the most often answered with a list of likes and dislikes. The interviewer is not asking for your taste. They want to see whether you can judge a product against what it is for, find the problems that matter, say how you know, and turn the judgment into a recommendation with a way to tell whether it worked.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: critique and &quot;favorite product&quot; questions at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others, often as a warm-up or as the second question in a product-sense round <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. On the job it is the work of studying a competitor, reviewing a team's own product with fresh eyes, or preparing to improve something (C08).</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether your critique is anchored, specific and prioritized <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for whether you start from the product's purpose and users, whether you walk a real flow and cite what you saw, whether you name strengths worth protecting, whether you rank problems and say why one matters more, whether your recommendation follows from the problem and has a metric, and whether you are honest about what you can and cannot know about a product you do not run.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You state what the product is for, and for whom, before judging it.</li>\n<li>You walk a specific flow and ground each point in what happens there.</li>\n<li>You name strengths, and say which to protect.</li>\n<li>You rank issues by importance with a stated reason.</li>\n<li>You recommend a change tied to the issue, with a metric and a risk.</li>\n<li>You separate what you observed, what you infer and what is your opinion, and you make no claim to inside knowledge.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end critique answers: a list of likes and dislikes with no goal, taste presented as user need, criticizing screens and not the product, a flat list with no ranking, a wish list of features as the recommendation, and claims about a company's metrics or intentions that the candidate cannot know.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c11-c11-1-what-a-critique-is-and-what-it-is-not\"></span>\n<h4>C11.1 What a critique is, and what it is not</h4>\n<p>A critique is a structured judgment of a product against its purpose, supported by evidence. A <strong>teardown</strong> is a structured analysis of how a product works and why, covering design, strategy and economics, from public information and use. A teardown describes and explains; a critique goes on to judge and recommend. Both are done from the outside, so both must be honest about what they cannot see.</p>\n<p><strong>What it is not.</strong> A review (does it deserve five stars?), a rant (what annoys me), a wish list (features I would add), or a design exercise (C07: design something new). It is also not an attack: the best critiques begin with what the product does well and why.</p>\n<p><strong>The core move.</strong> Judge relative to a goal. A feature that is a flaw for one purpose is a virtue for another: a product that hides settings behind menus is poor for power users and may be right for a mass-market tool whose job is to be simple. Without a stated goal, a critique is opinion; with one, it is an argument that someone can follow and dispute.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use the critique method.</strong> If asked to design something new, use C07. If asked how to improve a specific metric or area, use C08, which uses critique as input but adds sizing and constraints. If asked why a metric moved, use C13.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked to critique a food-delivery app, a candidate says: &quot;I like the clean design and the photos. I dislike the delivery fees and the long list of restaurants. I'd add a loyalty program and a recipe section.&quot; Each statement is a feeling or a feature; none is tied to a user, a task or a goal, and nothing is ranked. The interviewer asks, &quot;Which of those matters most, and how do you know?&quot; The candidate has no answer. A better opening: &quot;I'll judge it as a way for a busy household to get a hot meal on a weeknight with little effort. On that goal, the main problem is not the fees but the uncertainty about when the food will arrive.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. <em>The outsider's blind spot:</em> you do not see the constraints the team works under (regulation, contracts, technical limits, past failures), so a flaw you see may be a deliberate tradeoff. Say so, and ask what would explain the design. <em>Projecting yourself onto users:</em> your frustration is one data point, a hypothesis about a segment, not evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-c11-2-the-method-purpose-users-flow-strengths-ranked-issues-\"></span>\n<h4>C11.2 The method: purpose, users, flow, strengths, ranked issues, recommendations, metrics</h4>\n<p>FW-C11-01 is the spine: seven steps.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Purpose.</strong> State what the product is for, in a sentence: the job it does for the user and the goal it serves for the company, as you infer them from how it is offered and used (FW-C11-02). Name your assumption and invite correction.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Users.</strong> Name two or three <a href=\"#gl-user-segment\">user segment</a> groups by need and behavior, and say which one you critique for. A critique &quot;for everyone&quot; is a critique for no one.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Flow.</strong> Choose the key journey (the one that delivers the main value) and walk it, step by step, as the user would, including the first run. The <strong>first-run experience</strong> is what a person sees and does from first opening a product or device until they have reached their first real value, including setup, permissions and the first task. For a device it includes unboxing and pairing. Use C10's tools: the heuristic review and the state map.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Strengths.</strong> Name two or three things the product does well and why they matter for the goal. This earns credibility, shows that you understand the design, and marks what a change must not break (the legacy-user idea, C08).</p>\n<p><strong>5. Ranked issues.</strong> List the problems you found, each with the evidence (what you saw, in which step), the user it affects and the cost. Rank them (FW-C11-03) by severity (how bad when it happens), reach (how many people) and fixability. Say why the top issue is the top one. Treat accessibility and safety problems as requirements that outrank the score.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Recommendations.</strong> For the top one or two issues, recommend a change that follows from the problem (FW-C11-05): state the problem in the user's terms, choose a move (fix, add, remove, reposition, C08), say what it costs and what it risks.</p>\n<p><strong>7. Metrics.</strong> Say how you would know it worked: a primary metric tied to the problem, a guardrail, and what would change your mind.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The order forces the argument to be built before it is delivered: a goal, evidence, a ranking and a test. <strong>When not to use all seven steps at length.</strong> For a short question, give the purpose, one strength, the top issue with evidence, and a recommendation with a metric.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-c11-3-strategic-critique-business-model-distribution-defensi\"></span>\n<h4>C11.3 Strategic critique: business model, distribution, defensibility</h4>\n<p>Screens are not the whole product. A product succeeds or fails on whether the business around it works. FW-C11-04 adds a strategic layer to the critique.</p>\n<p><strong>Business model.</strong> Who pays, for what, and how much? What does a customer cost to acquire and serve, and what do they bring in? A product people love and that loses money on each customer is a problem in the model, not the interface (<a href=\"#gl-unit-economics\">unit economics</a>, C18).</p>\n<p><strong>Distribution.</strong> How do people find the product and start using it? A better product with no way to reach users loses to a worse product with a channel. Ask whether the product spreads by itself (sharing, invitations) or depends on paid acquisition.</p>\n<p><strong>Defensibility.</strong> Why would customers stay and rivals fail to copy it? Sources include a <a href=\"#gl-network-effect\">network effect</a> (the product is more valuable as more people use it), switching costs, unique data or content, scale economies and brand. A <a href=\"#gl-moat\">moat</a> (C18) is such an advantage. Features alone are easy to copy.</p>\n<p><strong>Timing and context.</strong> Does the product fit the moment: devices people have, habits they hold, rules in force, competitors' moves?</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A strategic critique shows that you understand why products win and lose beyond the interface. <strong>When not to lead with it.</strong> In a short critique of a specific flow, start with the user and the flow; add the strategic point as the reason the issue matters (&quot;it matters because the product depends on word of mouth&quot;).</p>\n<span id=\"c11-c11-4-from-critique-to-improvement\"></span>\n<h4>C11.4 From critique to improvement</h4>\n<p>A critique that ends at a list is half an answer. The bridge to improvement (FW-C11-05) has four moves.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pick one or two issues.</strong> The top-ranked. Resist &quot;I'd redesign everything.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Restate each as a problem</strong> for a user in a situation (<a href=\"#gl-problem-statement\">problem statement</a>, C06), without the solution in it.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose the move</strong> (fix, add, remove, reposition) and say why that one (C08, FW-C08-05), including what it could break.</li>\n<li><strong>Define the test and the metric</strong>, with a guardrail (C05, C14).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A recommendation should name its own uncertainty: &quot;I believe this because of what I saw; I would check it with five users and the funnel.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> It converts judgment into action that a team could take on Monday. <strong>When not to go further.</strong> If the interviewer asks only for a critique, stop at the ranked issues and a one-line recommendation.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-c11-5-live-critique-answer-architecture-including-the-favori\"></span>\n<h4>C11.5 Live-critique answer architecture, including the &quot;favorite product&quot; question</h4>\n<p>Section 4 sets out the live-critique answer with a time budget. The &quot;favorite product&quot; question deserves a note of its own (FW-C11-07), because it looks easier than it is.</p>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> &quot;What is your favorite product, and what would you change?&quot; It tests whether you can explain why a product is good in terms of a job, and whether you can criticize something you like without gushing or trashing it.</p>\n<p><strong>The approach.</strong> Choose a specific product you know well and use often, not an obvious flagship you cannot say anything new about. State the job it does for you and why it does it better than the alternatives (a strength with evidence). Then name what you would change: one issue, with evidence from your own use and a thought about who else feels it, a recommendation, a metric and a risk. Show you know the tradeoff you would be making.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfalls.</strong> Flattery (a list of praise); only complaint (a chance to show you cannot see value); choosing a product where your only reason is brand loyalty; a change that would help you and harm most users; and claiming knowledge of the company's internal data.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> It shows taste backed by reasoning, the core of product sense. <strong>When to adapt.</strong> If the interviewer names the product (for example, &quot;your favorite Apple product&quot;), use the same structure with the named product and say why it is your pick; do not make claims about the company's internal decisions.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-c11-6-worked-critiques-across-three-product-types-including-\"></span>\n<h4>C11.6 Worked critiques across three product types, including a device-and-software product</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives four worked critiques: a consumer app, a device-and-software product (wireless earbuds), a business tool and a strategic critique of a subscription service. Section 11 adds seven questions with model answers, including two on Apple products.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-c11-7-labeling-discipline-for-teardown-claims-verified-versu\"></span>\n<h4>C11.7 Labeling discipline for teardown claims: Verified versus Reconstructed</h4>\n<p>A critique of a real product mixes facts you can check with guesses. State which is which; the discipline also protects you from claims you cannot support (FW-C11-06). Two layers:</p>\n<p><strong>For facts about the product or company</strong> use the book's labels: <em>Verified</em> (from public documents or filings you can name), <em>Reconstructed</em> (inferred from public information, with the inference stated), <em>Illustrative</em> (invented to teach). Never state a real company's internal metrics, decisions or intentions as fact.</p>\n<p><strong>For claims in your own critique</strong> use three words: <em>observation</em> (&quot;when I pair the earbuds, the screen shows X&quot;), <em>inference</em> (&quot;this suggests the team prioritized speed over clarity&quot;), and <em>opinion</em> (&quot;I would prefer&quot;). An answer that says &quot;I observed this, I infer that, and I'd check it with users&quot; is more credible than one that states all three as fact.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> It makes the reasoning auditable and the claims defensible. <strong>When to relax it.</strong> In a spoken answer, a phrase (&quot;from using it,&quot; &quot;I'm guessing&quot;) is enough.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-c11-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h4>C11.8 Common mistakes</h4>\n<p>Section 8 lists ten common mistakes with how to detect each and the fix.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Seven frameworks, set out below. The heuristic review (FW-C10-01), the state map (FW-C10-02), the accessibility pass (FW-C10-03), the four moves (FW-C08-05), the product state card (FW-C08-02), the success-metric template (FW-C05-06) and the responsible-design check (FW-C07-06) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c11-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C11-01 · The critique spine · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a product and 15 to 30 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an answer with seven visible steps: purpose, users, flow, strengths, ranked issues, recommendations, metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can use the product or know it well from public information.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> structure makes the argument followable and can sound recited; adapt the order to the question.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> skipping the goal; a flat list of issues; a recommendation with no metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the critiques in section 5 and the model answers in section 11.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Purpose → Users → Flow → Strengths → Ranked issues → Recommendations → Metrics.</strong> The time budget is in section 4.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c11-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C11-02 · The goal-first statement · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a named product.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> one sentence: &quot;[Product] exists to help [user] do [job], and for the company it serves [goal]; I judge it by [criterion].&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the purpose can be inferred from public positioning and use; the interviewer will correct you.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> committing to a goal focuses the critique and can be wrong; say it is an assumption.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a goal that is a feature (&quot;to show recipes&quot;); a goal that flatters the product; no criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the statement for the saved-places critique in Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Template: <strong>&quot;X exists to help [segment] [do a job]. For the company it [earns / retains / grows]. I'll judge it on [how quickly and reliably users get that job done].&quot;</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c11-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C11-03 · The issue ranking grid · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a list of observed problems.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a table of issues with evidence, severity, reach and fixability, an impact score and a ranking, with accessibility and safety issues flagged as requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can rate each issue on a 1 to 5 scale from your use and reasoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> scores are judgments and can look precise; the reasons matter more than the numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> rating everything high; ignoring reach; letting a score bury an accessibility barrier.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the earbud issues in Worked example 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Issue</th>\n<th>Evidence</th>\n<th>Severity (1–5)</th>\n<th>Reach (1–5)</th>\n<th>Impact = S × R</th>\n<th>Fixability (1–5, 5 easy)</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<p>Rule: rank by impact; break ties by fixability; override the score for accessibility and safety problems, which are requirements.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c11-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C11-04 · The strategic lens · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a product and what is publicly known about how it makes money and reaches users.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> answers on business model, distribution, defensibility and timing, each labeled observation, inference or opinion, and the one that most explains the product's position.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> public information and your own use are enough for a first view.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> strategic critique is broader and less certain than flow critique.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> claiming internal numbers; a list of four items with no view on which matters; ignoring distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the subscription critique in Worked example 4 and the Quibi case in CS-C11-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Lens</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Business model</td>\n<td>Who pays, how much, and does each customer pay back their cost?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distribution</td>\n<td>How do people find and start using it, and at what cost?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Defensibility</td>\n<td>Why would customers stay and rivals fail to copy it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timing and context</td>\n<td>Does it fit the devices, habits and rules of the moment?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c11-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C11-05 · The critique-to-improvement bridge · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the top-ranked issue.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a problem statement without a solution, a chosen move (fix, add, remove, reposition) with a reason, a risk, a metric with a guardrail, and the cheapest test.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the issue is real for a segment you can name.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> focusing on one issue leaves others; say so.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a feature as the recommendation; no risk named; a metric the change cannot move.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the bridge in Worked example 3, and IQ-C11-03.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Issue → Problem statement → Move and why → What it could break → Metric and guardrail → Cheapest test.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c11-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C11-06 · The teardown claim labels · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a claim about a product or company you are about to make.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a label on each claim: for company facts, Verified, Reconstructed or Illustrative; for critique claims, observation, inference or opinion; and a rewrite of any claim you cannot support.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can tell what you know from what you guess.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> labels slow a fast answer slightly and make it more credible.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> stating guesses as facts; claiming internal knowledge; hiding opinion as observation.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the labeled claims in EX-C11-09 and the case studies' source notes.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Labels</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Company facts</td>\n<td>Verified (name the source type) · Reconstructed (state the inference) · Illustrative (invented, marked)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Your critique</td>\n<td>Observation (what I saw) · Inference (what I think it implies) · Opinion (what I prefer)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c11-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C11-07 · The favorite-product answer · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a product you know well and use.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a two-minute answer in five parts: the job, why it is the best at it, one thing to change with evidence, the recommendation with a metric and a risk, and the tradeoff you would be making.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can speak from real use.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a specific, modest pick is more interesting than an obvious flagship.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> praise only; complaint only; a change that serves you and harms most; claims about internal decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> IQ-C11-01 and EX-C11-07.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Job → Why it wins → One change (with evidence) → Metric and risk → The tradeoff.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"c11-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c11-the-live-critique-answer-for-a-20-minute-segment\"></span>\n<h4>The live-critique answer, for a 20-minute segment</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Purpose and users</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The goal-first statement; the segment you critique for</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flow</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The key journey walked step by step, including first run</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strengths</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Two or three, with why they matter and what to protect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ranked issues</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Three to five issues with evidence, severity and reach; the top one and why</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recommendation and metric</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The bridge: problem, move, risk, metric, guardrail, cheapest test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Close</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Two sentences; labels for what you know and infer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the interviewer names a flow, skip the choosing and spend more on the ranking. If you run behind, give the purpose, the top issue with evidence and a recommendation with a metric. Treat the interviewer's questions as steering.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> Lead with the goal: &quot;I'll judge this as a way for [user] to [job].&quot; Mark your claims: &quot;From using it, I noticed...; I'd guess that's because...; I'd check with users.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;The product does [strength] well, and its biggest problem for [user] is [issue], which costs them [cost]. I'd [change], measure [metric] with [guardrail], and I'd want to check [uncertainty].&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c11-type-critique-this-product\"></span>\n<h4>Type: critique this product</h4>\n<p>Use the full spine (FW-C11-01).</p>\n<span id=\"c11-type-favorite-product\"></span>\n<h4>Type: favorite product</h4>\n<p>Use FW-C11-07. Choose a specific product, keep the change modest and the tradeoff explicit.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-type-critique-for-strategy\"></span>\n<h4>Type: critique for strategy</h4>\n<p>Use the strategic lens (FW-C11-04) alongside the flow: why the product is where it is.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-type-device-and-software\"></span>\n<h4>Type: device and software</h4>\n<p>Include unboxing, setup and the first run, and the states that a physical device has (indicator lights, sounds, pairing, a second device).</p>\n<span id=\"c11-type-the-short-version-8-to-10-minutes\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the short version (8 to 10 minutes)</h4>\n<p>Goal, one strength, the top issue with evidence, recommendation with a metric. Protect the ranking.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Who is this for?&quot;</td>\n<td>You skipped the user</td>\n<td>State the segment and the job</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Why is that the most important issue?&quot;</td>\n<td>Your ranking is unclear</td>\n<td>Give severity, reach and the alternative you rejected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How do you know?&quot;</td>\n<td>Opinion stated as fact</td>\n<td>Say what you observed and what you infer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Maybe they did that on purpose.&quot;</td>\n<td>The outsider's blind spot</td>\n<td>Name the likely tradeoff and say what you would check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What would you do about it?&quot;</td>\n<td>You stopped at the problem</td>\n<td>Give the bridge: problem, move, risk, metric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How would you measure that?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want a metric</td>\n<td>Use the C05 template with a guardrail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What do you like about it?&quot;</td>\n<td>You skipped strengths</td>\n<td>Name two with why they matter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c11-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All four examples are <code>Illustrative</code>. Where a real product type is the setting (examples 1 and 2), nothing here is a claim about a real company's metrics, systems or decisions, and every figure is an invented assumption; observations are described as what a user could see. The arithmetic was computed. Each follows the critique spine (FW-C11-01) and is written as notes you could say aloud.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-worked-example-1-consumer-app-critiquing-saved-places-in-a-m\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Consumer app: critiquing &quot;saved places&quot; in a maps product (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a maps product of the kind Google operates. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Purpose and users (FW-C11-02).</strong> Saved places exist to help travelers and planners remember places and find them again when they need them. For the company, saved places keep people returning and feed useful signals about interest (an assumption, labeled as inference). I'll judge it by how quickly and reliably a person gets from &quot;I saved this once&quot; to &quot;I'm here and need it.&quot; I'll critique for a traveler on a week-long trip who saved 30 places in advance.</p>\n<p><strong>Flow.</strong> Find a place, tap save, put it in a list, later open the list, pick a place, navigate, and perhaps share the list with a friend.</p>\n<p><strong>Strengths.</strong> One tap to save, which makes saving nearly free (so people save a lot). Integration with search and navigation, so a saved place is one tap from directions. These are worth protecting: any change that adds steps to saving would hurt.</p>\n<p><strong>Ranked issues (FW-C11-03).</strong> Scores are my judgments (invented).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Issue</th>\n<th>Evidence (observation)</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Impact</th>\n<th>Fixability</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>On the trip, 30 saved places appear as pins and lists with no sense of &quot;near me now&quot; or &quot;open now&quot;</td>\n<td>Opening the list shows a long list sorted by date saved</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A saved place that has closed permanently looks the same as an open one</td>\n<td>Pins carry no status until opened</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sharing a list gives unclear permission (can the friend edit?)</td>\n<td>The share screen does not say</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many lists with similar names after a few trips</td>\n<td>Default list names repeat</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The top issue is the first: saving is easy and retrieval at the moment of need is hard. That is the gap between the stated purpose and the experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Inference, not observation.</strong> My guess is that people save far more places than they visit; I'd check by looking at how many saved places are ever visited. I cannot know the actual numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation (FW-C11-05).</strong> Problem: &quot;A traveler with many saved places cannot quickly see which are near, open and worth going to now, so saved places go unused.&quot; Move: an <em>add</em> that is mostly a <em>fix</em>: a &quot;near me&quot; view of saved places, filtered by open now and sorted by distance, offered when the person is in a city where they have saved places. Risk: noise (a notification for every saved place would annoy), so show it only on request or at a moderate prompt. Metric: the share of saved places visited within 90 days (say 18% today, a target of 30%, invented), with a guardrail on the share of users who turn off saved-place suggestions, and a counter metric on saves per user (a drop would show that the feature makes saving feel heavy). Test: show the view to a random 5% of users in travel cities (C14), and meanwhile watch five travelers use it.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The critique ranked by impact, named strengths to protect, labeled inference, and ended with a recommendation tied to a metric.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-worked-example-2-device-and-software-critiquing-the-first-ru\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Device and software: critiquing the first run of wireless earbuds (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: wireless earbuds sold with a phone app, of the kind Apple and several other companies make. No claim is made about any real product. Platform guidelines are a public reference <code>[V: Apple Human Interface Guidelines and Material Design]</code>.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Purpose and users.</strong> The earbuds exist to take a new owner from the box to listening in about a minute and keep them listening. Segment: first-time owners using a phone they already own; the switchers who already have another pair are a second segment. The first-run experience is the product's first impression and the point where returns begin.</p>\n<p><strong>Flow (device and software).</strong> Open the box, power the earbuds on, pair with the phone, set up optional features (a fit test, controls, voice assistant, a companion app), listen for the first time. For devices the states include indicator lights, sounds and what the case shows.</p>\n<p><strong>Strengths.</strong> Pairing that starts when the case is opened near the phone (a pattern several makers use) makes the first step nearly effortless. A voice prompt confirming &quot;connected&quot; gives clear feedback when the user's eyes are elsewhere. Both are worth protecting.</p>\n<p><strong>Ranked issues (FW-C11-03).</strong> Scores are my judgments (invented).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Issue</th>\n<th>Observation</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Impact</th>\n<th>Fixability</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>The ear-tip fit test, which affects sound quality, is hidden several screens into the app</td>\n<td>Most people never find it</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>If pairing fails there is no message, only a blinking light</td>\n<td>The user cannot tell whether to wait, retry or reset</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connecting a second device (a laptop) has no obvious path</td>\n<td>Users search settings</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The meaning of the case's light colors is not explained</td>\n<td>Users guess</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Setup is visual only; a low-vision user has no spoken guidance for the steps</td>\n<td>The app screens carry the instructions</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Reading the grid.</strong> By score, the order is the fit test (15), failed pairing (10), second device (9), the light colors (8), and the spoken setup guidance (5). But the last is an accessibility barrier: for the people it affects, setup is impossible without help. Accessibility and safety are requirements that override the score (FW-C11-03), so spoken guidance goes into the fix list regardless of its reach. A device adds a constraint a screen does not: some behaviors (the case's lights and sounds) are in hardware or firmware that cannot be changed after shipping, so I'd separate fixes that can be made in the app from those that cannot (C10, C17).</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> Problem: &quot;A first-time owner does not find the fit test and gets no guidance when pairing fails, so some have poor sound and some return the product.&quot; Moves: a <em>fix</em> that surfaces the fit test as an offered step right after the first connection, and a <em>fix</em> that adds a plain failure message with a &quot;try again / reset&quot; instruction in the app and a distinct sound from the earbuds. Risk: a longer first run for people who want to skip; make the fit test optional and skippable. Metrics: primary, the share of new owners who reach a first listening session within five minutes of opening the box; input, the share who complete the fit test; guardrails: pairing success rate, support contacts and returns in the first month; counter metric: the share who skip setup entirely. A caution: returns and support contacts are lagging signals, so I would use the five-minute measure as a proxy and check that it predicts fewer returns (C14, FW-C14-08).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> For a device, the first run spans hardware and software, accessibility can override a scoring grid, and some fixes are only possible before the product ships.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-worked-example-3-business-tool-critiquing-the-onboarding-of-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — Business tool: critiquing the onboarding of a project-management product (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Purpose and users.</strong> The product helps teams plan and track work; the company earns by seat, so it grows when more teammates join. The user I critique for is the <em>admin</em>, the first person at a company to try it, who decides whether the team adopts it. The buyer, the admin and the daily user can be different people (C08, FW-C08-02).</p>\n<p><strong>Flow and numbers (invented).</strong> 10,000 admins sign up in a month. 8,500 create a workspace (85%); 5,100 create a first project (60% of those); 2,800 invite at least three teammates (55% of those). So the activation rate, an admin who has created a project and invited three people, is 2,800 of 10,000, or 28% (<a href=\"#gl-activation\">activation</a>, C05).</p>\n<p><strong>Strengths.</strong> A workspace opens with a sample project that shows what the product does (value before effort). Worth protecting.</p>\n<p><strong>Ranked issues.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Issue</th>\n<th>Observation</th>\n<th>Impact on the goal</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>After creating a workspace, a blank screen offers no guidance to start a first project</td>\n<td>40% of admins stop here (3,400 people)</td>\n<td>Large leak at the largest step</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inviting teammates asks for email addresses one at a time, with no link to share or import</td>\n<td>45% stop here (2,300 people)</td>\n<td>The growth step: teammates are the way the product spreads</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The first project template list is long and unlabeled</td>\n<td>Admins scroll and leave</td>\n<td>Adds to the first leak</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Judgment on priority.</strong> By raw numbers the blank first screen loses more people (3,400 against 2,300). But the invite step is the product's growth loop: each admin who invites three people brings more seats, and the invite step is cheap to fix (a shareable link, import from contacts). The arithmetic of a fix: raising the invite step from 55% to 65% gives 5,100 × 0.65 = 3,315 activated admins, 33.2% against 28%, a gain of 5.2 points. Raising the project step from 60% to 70% gives 8,500 × 0.70 = 5,950 who create a project and 5,950 × 0.55 = 3,272 who invite, 32.7%, a gain of 4.7 points. Both are worth doing; I'd start with the invite step because it is cheaper and it feeds growth, and I'd say the ranking would flip if the blank-screen fix were cheaper.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> A shareable invite link and a &quot;paste a list of emails&quot; field, with a gentle prompt to invite after the first project. Metric: activation rate, with a guardrail on the share of invited teammates who report the invitation as unwanted (spam-like), since easier inviting can annoy people, and a counter metric on teammates who accept and never use the product.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Ranking by the goal (activation and growth) and not only by the biggest leak, with the arithmetic to support it and a guardrail against an unwanted side effect.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-worked-example-4-strategic-critique-a-meal-kit-subscription-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Strategic critique: a meal-kit subscription (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Purpose and users.</strong> A weekly subscription that delivers ingredients and recipes for people who want to cook at home without planning or shopping. The critique here is strategic (FW-C11-04): not what is wrong with the screens, but whether the business works.</p>\n<p><strong>Business model (invented).</strong> An order averages $70 with a 25% contribution margin, so $17.50 per order; customers order twice a month, so $35 of contribution a month. Acquiring a customer costs $90 through paid advertising. Payback: $90 ÷ $35 = 2.6 months. Monthly churn is 12%, so the expected lifetime is 1 ÷ 0.12 = 8.3 months and lifetime contribution is about $292. The ratio of lifetime contribution to acquisition cost is 3.2: a healthy business on these numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>The sensitivity.</strong> The model is fragile where churn is concerned. At 20% monthly churn the lifetime is 5 months, lifetime contribution $175 and the ratio 1.9. If the cost of acquiring a customer also rises to $140 (as the cheapest audiences are used up), the ratio falls to 1.25, a thin margin for error, before accounting for the cost of support and wasted food. The strategic question is churn: why do customers leave, and can the product change it?</p>\n<p><strong>Distribution.</strong> Mostly paid advertising and promotions, as inferred from the pricing of first boxes. That means growth costs money in proportion to new customers, and referrals and word of mouth are the cheaper channels to build.</p>\n<p><strong>Defensibility.</strong> Weak, as I see it: switching costs are low (a customer cancels with a click and tries a competitor's discount), there is no network effect, and recipes and ingredients are not unique. Possible sources: a supply chain advantage, a brand, personalization from taste data. I would label these as inference.</p>\n<p><strong>Timing and context.</strong> If demand rose in periods when people stayed home and fell afterward, that can mean the product served a moment as much as a habit (a hypothesis to check against public coverage of the category).</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> Treat retention as the central product problem: study why people cancel, make skipping a week easy (so a busy week pauses and does not cancel), and invest in what makes the box part of a routine. Metrics: retention at 90 days by acquisition channel, with a guardrail on contribution margin per order and a counter metric on the rate of paused-then-cancelled customers.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A product can have a good interface and a fragile business; the strategic layer found where it was fragile and tied the product work (skipping, routine) to the number that mattered (churn).</p>\n<span id=\"c11-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Critique a to-do list app.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c11-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I like that it's simple and the checkboxes feel satisfying. I don't like that there are too many menus and no dark mode. I'd add a calendar view and reminders.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> likes and dislikes with no goal, user or ranking, and a list of features as the recommendation. Nothing says why any of it matters.</p>\n<span id=\"c11-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd judge it as a way for a busy professional to keep track of what they must do and actually do it. For that user, I'd walk the main flow: capture a task, organize it, see today's list, complete it.</p>\n<p>&quot;Strengths: capturing is fast, and the today view is clear. I'd protect both. Issues, ranked by how much they get in the way of the goal: first, tasks pile up with no sense of priority, so the today list grows beyond what a person can do, which defeats the purpose; second, recurring tasks are awkward to set up; third, the menus are deep. The top one matters most because it is the reason people abandon to-do apps: the list becomes a source of guilt.</p>\n<p>&quot;Recommendation: help people choose what to do today, for example by asking at the start of the day for the three that matter, and moving the rest out of view. Metric: the share of tasks planned for a day that are completed that day, with a guardrail on daily retention. I'd test it with five users and in a staged rollout.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c11-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd say what I observed against what I infer. I observed that the today list has no cap; I infer that the team optimized for capture, because the product's early growth came from people saving lots of tasks; I can't know that. I'd also be careful that a limit feels like a loss for heavy users, so I'd make it a suggestion, not a rule, and measure heavy users separately (the legacy-user point).</p>\n<p>&quot;On strategy: to-do apps are easy to copy and switch from, so the defensibility is in habit and data: if a person's whole life is in the list, leaving is costly. That suggests retention and import/export matter more than another feature, and makes the guilt problem a retention problem. The business probably depends on converting free users to paid, so a change that improves completion should be checked against what drives conversion.</p>\n<p>&quot;The risk of my recommendation is that asking people to choose three tasks adds friction to the first minute of the day, so I'd test it as optional first. What would change my mind: if data showed that people who accumulate long lists still retain well, then the pile-up is not the problem and the issue is elsewhere, perhaps recurring tasks.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c11-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>States a goal and a user, walks a flow, ranks issues</td>\n<td>Separates observation, inference and opinion; names the tradeoff and the blind spot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Names strengths to protect</td>\n<td>Adds the strategic layer: business, distribution, defensibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ties a recommendation to a metric and a test</td>\n<td>Names the risk of the recommendation and what would change the view</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c11-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple designs hardware, software and services together and publishes platform design guidelines <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple Human Interface Guidelines (developer.apple.com) and privacy pages; Apple's own description of its platforms and services]</code>. Third-party guides report that &quot;favorite product&quot; and first-run critique questions are common at Apple and reward specific, detail-aware answers <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: choose a specific product, walk the first run across hardware and software, cover accessibility and the user's other devices, and make no claim about internal decisions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google publishes design guidance and a stated philosophy that begins with the user <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google, Ten things we know to be true; Material Design]</code>. Candidates commonly report critique prompts about its own products at large scale <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: critique for a specific segment, include users with older devices and slow connections, and say how a change would be tested at scale. Appendix A has briefs on eight Google products to practice against.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon publishes Customer Obsession as a leadership principle, and former leaders describe writing a customer-facing press release and FAQ before building <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles; Amazon working-backwards material]</code>. Candidates commonly report that critiques are expected to start from the customer's problem <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: state the customer's job and the customer-visible cost of each issue.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Candidates commonly report critique prompts about social products, with follow-ups on engagement, safety and unintended effects <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: include the effects on other users and on people who are not the target segment, and apply the ethical nudge test (C10) to engagement mechanics.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft publishes inclusive-design principles and its products often serve buyers, administrators and daily users who differ <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft Inclusive Design toolkit manual (Microsoft Design)]</code> <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: say whose experience you critique, and include accessibility and the administrator's view.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix publishes material on its culture and on personalization and testing <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo; Netflix Tech Blog]</code>; candidates report data-informed prompts <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: say what you would test to confirm a critique, and separate what people say from what they do.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe is widely described as emphasizing documentation and developer experience <code>Inferred</code> <code>[V: Stripe documentation reputation: Slab interview with its former Documentation Manager and Mintlify article; design principles for checkout not separately checked]</code>. Practical consequence: for developer products, critique the first integration, the documentation and the error messages as the interface.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c11-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>No stated goal</td>\n<td>You list likes and dislikes</td>\n<td>Open with the goal-first statement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Critiquing for everyone</td>\n<td>&quot;Users&quot; with no segment</td>\n<td>Name one segment and its job</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Screen-level taste</td>\n<td>Color and style opinions</td>\n<td>Tie each point to a task and a cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>A flat list of issues</td>\n<td>No ranking, no reason for the top</td>\n<td>Rank by severity and reach; justify the top</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Skipping strengths</td>\n<td>Only complaints</td>\n<td>Name two strengths and what to protect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>A feature wish list as the recommendation</td>\n<td>&quot;Add X, Y, Z&quot;</td>\n<td>State the problem, then choose a move</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Claiming inside knowledge</td>\n<td>&quot;They obviously optimize for...&quot;</td>\n<td>Label as inference; say what you would check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Ignoring the business</td>\n<td>Only interface points for a failing business</td>\n<td>Add the strategic lens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>A recommendation with no metric or risk</td>\n<td>The change cannot be tested</td>\n<td>Add a metric, a guardrail and a risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Gushing or trashing a favorite</td>\n<td>All praise or all complaint</td>\n<td>Specific strength, one modest change, the tradeoff</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c11-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. The legacy-user idea is from C08.</p>\n<p><strong>Steelman first</strong> (<code>MM-steelman</code>). Before criticizing a design, build the strongest case for why it is that way: a tradeoff, a constraint, a different goal. A flaw that survives the steelman is a real flaw; one that does not was a misunderstanding. It is the critic's version of asking why a fence was built before removing it (C08).</p>\n";
